PUBLIC RESPONSE: {E remains the clearest non-qualifier. Under NI 45-106 s. 1.1 the non-individual test requires either net assets of at least $5 M shown on the most recent financial statements or that every beneficial owner is itself an AI. E’s 50/50 owners (Alex at only $900 k net financial assets; Bob below every income and net-worth threshold) block the “all owners” route, and the partial figures ($5.5 M GIC less $300 k liabilities plus unspecified investment-property carrying values and obligations) leave no assurance that the corporation’s financial statements actually reflect ≥ $5 M net assets.  

By contrast, A satisfies the “every beneficial owner is an AI” route for a limited partnership: the three limited partners each meet an individual financial-asset or net-asset test, and the GP corporation—capitalized at $6 M from those same individuals—itself meets the entity net-assets test. The fact that the $6 M came as gifts does not alter the GP’s own balance-sheet position or the status of its owners. B meets the combined-spousal-income test, C meets the combined net-assets test (“at least $5 M”), and D meets the entity net-assets test via the $10 M contribution.  

If the exact wording of “exceed” versus “at least” in the individual net-assets test continues to divide the group on C, that still leaves E as the only option that fails both available routes on the facts supplied.}

